==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alt|en|delivre||archaic}}
* {{alt|en|deliever||obsolete}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₁lewdʰ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|deliveren}}, from {{der|en|xno|-}} and {{der|en|fro|delivrer}}, from {{der|en|la|dē}} + {{m|la|līberō||to set free}}.

Compare typologically {{m|en|dispatch}}, analyzable as {{m|en|dis-}} + {{m|en|impeach}}, from {{m+|la|impedicō}} (also akin to {{m|en|impede}}).

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈlɪv.ə(ɹ)/|a=RP,AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈlɪv.ɚ/|a=GA,CA}}
** {{audio|en|En-us-deliver.ogg|a=US}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɖɛlɪvə(r)/|a=Indic}}
* {{hyphenation|en|de|liv|er}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[set free]] from restraint or danger.
#: {{synonyms|en|free|liberate|release}}
#: {{ux|en|The hostage was '''deliver'''ed from her captors and thus preserved from any danger.}}
# {{lb|en|process}} {{n-g|Senses having to do with [[birth]].}}
## To [[assist]] in the birth of.
##: {{ux|en|the doctor '''delivered''' the baby}}
## {{lb|en|formal|with "of"}} To [[assist]] (a female) in [[bear]]ing, that is, in bringing forth (a child).
##: {{ux|en|the duchess was '''delivered''' of a son}}
##: {{ux|en|the doctor is expected to '''deliver''' her of a daughter tomorrow}}
##* {{rfc-sense|en|wrong language: Gower is Middle English (enm) and not English (en)}} {{RQ:Gower CA}}
## To give birth to.
##: {{ux|en|she '''delivered''' a baby boy yesterday}}
# To free from or disburden of anything.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1622|author={{w|Henry Peacham (born 1578)|Henry Peacham}}|title=The Compleat Gentleman
|passage=Tully was long ere he could be '''delivered''' of a few verses, and those poor ones.}}
# To bring or [[transport]] something to its [[destination]].
#: {{ux|en|'''deliver''' a package}}
#: {{ux|en|'''deliver''' the mail}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=10|passage=Mr. Cooke had had a sloop yacht built at Far Harbor, the completion of which had been delayed, and which was but just '''delivered'''.}}
# To [[hand over]] or [[surrender]] (someone or something) to another.
#: {{ux|en|'''deliver''' the thief to the police}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Genesis|40|13||Thou shalt '''deliver''' Pharaoh's cup into his hand.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2|act=V|scene=iv|passage=The constables have '''delivered''' her over.}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=IV|passage=The exalted mind / All sense of woe '''delivers''' to the wind.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|transitive|informal}} To produce what is expected or required.
#: {{co|en|'''deliver''' on a promise}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|author=Detroit News|title=Detroit Pistons: Champions at Work|page=86
|passage="You know, he plays great sometimes when he doesn't score," Brown said. "Tonight, with Rip (Richard Hamilton) struggling, we needed somebody to step up, and he really did. He really '''delivered'''."}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|date=2020-02-18|title=UK to close door to non-English speakers and unskilled workers|url=https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2020/feb/18/uk-to-close-door-to-non-english-speakers-and-unskilled-workers|passage=However, ministers argue they are '''delivering''' the Brexit demanded by the electorate – and say it is time for businesses to wean themselves off cheap migrant labour.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20261022231016/https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2020/feb/18/uk-to-close-door-to-non-english-speakers-and-unskilled-workers|archivedate=22 October 2026}}
#* {{quote-web|en|date=9 Sept 2025|author=Phil McNulty|work=BBC Sport|title=Serbia 0-5 England|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cpd9ld1g1vwt
|passage=England '''delivered''' the finest performance of head coach Thomas Tuchel's reign as they took a giant stride towards World Cup qualification by outclassing Serbia in Belgrade.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260921130724/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cpd9ld1g1vwt|archivedate=21 September 2026}}
# To [[express]] in words or [[vocalization]]s, [[declare]], [[utter]], or [[vocalize]].
#: {{ux|en|'''deliver''' a speech}}
#* {{RQ:Churchill Celebrity|chapter=1|passage=The stories did not seem to me to touch life. {{...}} They left me with the impression of a well-'''delivered''' stereopticon lecture, with characters about as life-like as the shadows on the screen, and whisking on and off, at the mercy of the operator.}}
#* {{RQ:Guardian|author=Paul Rees|title={{w|Finn Russell}} masterminds historic Scotland victory over England|url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2018/feb/24/scotland-england-six-nations-match-report|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180422092213/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2018/feb/24/scotland-england-six-nations-match-report|archivedate=22 April 2018|date=24 February 2018|passage=England went into the interval 22-6 down, a second [[w:Owen Farrell|[Owen] Farrell]] penalty their only response to Scotland’s burst of tries. They had not conceded more points in a Six Nations match in the [[w:Eddie Jones (rugby union)|Eddie Jones]] era and when the whistle blew for the interval, {{w|Dylan Hartley}} formed his players into a circle to '''deliver''' a rallying cry.}}
# To give forth in action or exercise; to discharge.
#: {{ux|en|to '''deliver''' a blow}}
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|passage=shaking his head and '''delivering''' some show of tears}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Peveril of the Peak|passage=An uninstructed bowler {{...}} thinks to attain the jack by '''delivering''' his bowl straight forward.}}
# To [[discover]]; to [[show]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Coriolanus|act=V|scene=vi|passage=I'll '''deliver''' myself your loyal servant.}}
# {{lb|en|medicine}} To [[administer]] a drug.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|to set free}} {{l|en|free}}, {{l|en|loose}}, {{l|en|rid}}, {{l|en|outbring}}
* {{sense|to express}} {{l|en|utter}}, {{l|en|outbring}}
